  "raw": {
    "https://cuny.jobs/6165C952D80248009FB3C35412665A5925": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Black Studies/Human Geography (Tenure-Track)",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Black Studies/Human Geography (Tenure-Track). Assistant Professor of Black Studies/Human Geography (Tenure-Track) **FACULTY VACANCY ANNOUNCEMENT** **Position Details** The Department of Interdisciplinary Arts & Sciences at the City College Center for Worker Education (CUNY) invites applications for a Tenure-Track Assistant Professor of Black Studies/Human Geography to start in Fall 2027. Candidates for this position should be active in research, teaching, and community engagement, with a focus on the Americas and African diasporas. Areas of expertise could include policing and security, mapping, urban studies, environmental studies/sustainability, and applied social sciences, digital social sciences. The successful candidate will be eager and able to collaborate across divisions and programs within City College. The department offers an interdisciplinary BA program in Liberal Arts, a BA in Social Justice and Urban Life, a BS in Early Childhood Education for working adults, an MA in the Study of the Americas, and two new MA programs in Instructional Design and International and Transcultural Education. The successful candidate will have a range of responsibilities, including teaching 4-5 courses [18 Credit hours] per year, working with the MA students, and participating in the development of a growing curriculum in Black Studies and Social Sciences. They will also be expected to teach, mentor, and advise both undergraduate and graduate students, and to build community partnerships. Expected duties include: + Teaching, research, and guidance duties in area(s) of expertise noted above. + Sharing responsibility for committee and department assignments. + Performing administrative and supervisory tasks as may be assigned. **QUALIFICATIONS** Ph.D. degree in compatible area of Social Sciences or equivalent. Also required are the ability to teach successfully, demonstrated scholarship or achievement, and ability to cooperate with others for the good of the institution. **Preferred Qualifications** + Ph.D. degree in a compatible area of Social Sciences or compatible Humanities and Interdisciplinary Studies. + Applicants with experience teaching adult, non-traditional urban students are especially encouraged. **COMPENSATION** **Salary Range:** $79,630 - $94,017 CUNY offers faculty a competitive compensation and benefits package covering health insurance, pension and retirement benefits, paid parental leave, and savings programs. We also provide mentoring and support for research, scholarship, and publication as part of our commitment to ongoing faculty professional development. **HOW TO APPLY** **Only applications submitted through CUNYfirst will be considered for this position.** If you are viewing this job posting in CUNYfirst, please click on \"Apply Now\" on the bottom of this page and follow the instructions. If you are viewing this job posting externally, please apply as follows: + Go to https://cuny.jobs/ + Search for Job Opening ID number **32788** + Click on the \"Apply Now\" button and follow the instructions. Applications, including the following must be uploaded to the CUNYfirst job application website as a single PDF document: 1. Statement of scholarly interests 2. Curriculum Vitae / Resume 3. Names and contact information for three professional references **CLOSING DATE** November 22, 2026 **JOB SEARCH CATEGORY** CUNY Job Posting: Faculty **EQUAL EMPLOYMENT OPPORTUNITY** CUNY encourages people with disabilities, minorities, veterans and women to apply. At CUNY, Italian Americans are also included among our protected groups. Applicants and employees will not be discriminated against on the basis of any legally protected category, including sexual orientation or gender identity. EEO/AA/Vet/Disability Employer. Job ID: 32788 Location: City College of New York Job Type: Full-Time"
    },
    "https://cuny.jobs/EB7F08EC8C4743F7BADD99884433EE0025": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Special Education",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Special Education. Assistant Professor of Special Education **FACULTY VACANCY ANNOUNCEMENT** The School of Education at The City College of New York invites applications for a full-time, Assistant Professor position in Special Education to begin in the Fall of 2027. The position requires service to the department and college and academic advisement. The successful candidate is expected to collaborate with colleagues to seek external funding, to collaborate with local school districts, to teach courses in the Special Education program, and to participate in committee work. **QUALIFICATIONS** Ph.D. or Ed.D. Special Education or equivalent. Also required are the ability to teach successfully, demonstrated scholarship or achievement, and ability to cooperate with others for the good of the institution. Preferred Qualifications + Experience teaching face-to-face, online, and hybrid courses. + Experience teaching undergraduate and graduate courses. + Commitment to urban education. + Experience teaching or working in an official capacity in public schools. **COMPENSATION** **Salary Range:** $79,630 - $94,017 CUNY offers faculty a competitive compensation and benefits package covering health insurance, pension and retirement benefits, paid parental leave, and savings programs. We also provide mentoring and support for research, scholarship, and publication as part of our commitment to ongoing faculty professional development. **HOW TO APPLY** **Only applications submitted through CUNYfirst will be considered for this position.** If you are viewing this job posting in CUNYfirst, please click on \"Apply Now\" on the bottom of this page and follow the instructions. If you are viewing this job posting externally, please apply as follows: + Go to https://cuny.jobs/ + Search for Job Opening ID number **32797** + Click on the \"Apply Now\" button and follow the instructions. Applications, including the following must be uploaded to the CUNYfirst job application website as a single PDF document: + Letter of Interest: This letter should include career highlights, teaching experience, and alignment with CCNYs mission. We encourage applicants to discuss how their scholarship and/or teaching philosophies demonstrate a commitment to fostering inclusive approaches to teaching and mentoring pre-service and in-service teachers. + CV; + List of three references with contact information; + Samples of other expository writing are welcome but not required. **CLOSING DATE** November 2, 2026 **JOB SEARCH CATEGORY** CUNY Job Posting: Faculty **EQUAL EMPLOYMENT OPPORTUNITY** CUNY encourages people with disabilities, minorities, veterans and women to apply. At CUNY, Italian Americans are also included among our protected groups. Applicants and employees will not be discriminated against on the basis of any legally protected category, including sexual orientation or gender identity. EEO/AA/Vet/Disability Employer. Job ID: 32797 Location: City College of New York Job Type: Full-Time"
    },
    "https://cuny.jobs/DD7F80753EF74434ABBA3C01F8AE21BA25": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of English (Composition and Writing Studies)",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of English (Composition and Writing Studies). Assistant Professor of English (Composition and Writing Studies) **FACULTY VACANCY ANNOUNCEMENT** **Position Details** The Department of English at The City College of New York (CUNY) invites applications for a full-time tenure- track Assistant Professor in Composition and Writing Studies, with a particular specialization in multilingual writing pedagogies. Additional experience in one or more of the following areas is also preferred: literacy studies; language studies; adult teaching and learning; basic writing; translingual writing; digital literacies; critical AI studies; disability studies; service learning; and writing program administration and assessment. The position will begin in the Fall of 2027. We seek candidates with an established and growing record of publication and evidence of effective teaching at the college level. The successful candidate will be expected to teach classes at both the undergraduate and graduate levels, including first-year writing courses, upper-division courses in language, writing, and rhetoric, and graduate courses in our MA in Language & Literacy an interdisciplinary program that prepares future teachers of adults in diverse educational programs. The standard workload for this position is 18 credits per year (a 3/3 teaching load). The candidate will also be expected to maintain an active agenda of publication and participate in student mentoring and departmental and college service activities in a large public university setting. We seek applicants motivated to collaborate with a strong team of creative writing and literature faculty in a thriving Hispanic- and Minority-Serving Institution located in Harlem. For more information on our English Department, please visit: https://english.ccny.cuny.edu/. **QUALIFICATIONS** Ph.D. in composition/writing studies, or a related field. Also required are the ability to teach successfully, demonstrated scholarship or achievement, and ability to cooperate with others for the good of the institution. **Preferred Qualifications** + a Ph.D. in composition/writing studies or related discipline in hand by August 1, 2027 + Evidence of a productive research agenda in composition/writing studies + Demonstrable experience and success in teaching at the college or university level **COMPENSATION** **Salary Range:** $79,630 - $94,017 CUNY offers faculty a competitive compensation and benefits package covering health insurance, pension and retirement benefits, paid parental leave, and savings programs. We also provide mentoring and support for research, scholarship, and publication as part of our commitment to ongoing faculty professional development. **HOW TO APPLY** **Only applications submitted through CUNYfirst will be considered for this position.** If you are viewing this job posting in CUNYfirst, please click on \"Apply Now\" on the bottom of this page and follow the instructions. If you are viewing this job posting externally, please apply as follows: + Go to https://cuny.jobs/ + Search for Job Opening ID number **32844** + Click on the \"Apply Now\" button and follow the instructions. Applications, including the following must be uploaded to the CUNYfirst job application website as a single PDF document: + Cover letter (1-2 pages) discussing scholarly research agenda, teaching and service experience, and interest in the position + Curriculum vitae + Representative writing sample of scholarship (25 pages maximum) + Teaching portfolio that includes at least two teaching evaluations (reports from faculty observers or survey questionnaire reports from students), at least two samples of syllabi used in recently taught courses, and a statement of teaching philosophy + Names and contact information for three recommenders who may be asked to submit a letter in support of the application **CLOSING DATE** November 24, 2026 **JOB SEARCH CATEGORY** CUNY Job Posting: Faculty **EQUAL EMPLOYMENT OPPORTUNITY** CUNY encourages people with disabilities, minorities, veterans and women to apply. At CUNY, Italian Americans are also included among our protected groups. Applicants and employees will not be discriminated against on the basis of any legally protected category, including sexual orientation or gender identity. EEO/AA/Vet/Disability Employer. Job ID: 32844 Location: City College of New York Job Type: Full-Time"
    },
    "https://cuny.jobs/94F60EB07E0E4AAC89742C307D982EFF25": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor â€“ History of Science and Technology in Asia",
      "description": "Assistant Professor â€“ History of Science and Technology in Asia. Assistant Professor History of Science and Technology in Asia **FACULTY VACANCY ANNOUNCEMENT** **Position Details** The Department of History at The City College of New York (CCNY) invites applications for a tenure-track position at the Assistant Professor level in the History of Science and Technology in Asia. Geographic and chronological specialization within Asia is open. We welcome scholars whose research situates science and technology within broader social, cultural, political, economic, environmental, or transnational contexts, including candidates whose work examines connections between Asia and other regions of the world. Teaching responsibilities include courses in the history of science and technology, Asian history, undergraduate and M.A. electives in the candidates areas of expertise, and classes that contribute to the colleges general education curriculum. **About The City College of New York** For over 175 years, The City College of New York has provided access to high-quality education for New Yorkers in a wide variety of disciplines. Over 17,000 students pursue undergraduate and graduate degrees in the College of Liberal Arts and Sciences, the School of Architecture, the School of Education, the Grove School of Engineering, the Center for Worker Education, the Colin Powell School for Civic and Global Leadership, and the Sophie Davis School of Biomedical Education. The City College of New York has produced ten Nobel laureates. **QUALIFICATIONS** Ph.D. in History or a related field. Also required are the ability to teach successfully, demonstrated scholarly achievement, and ability to cooperate with others for the good of the institution. **Preferred Qualifications** + Evidence of excellent scholarly potential; + Demonstrated ability to conduct research using the languages appropriate to the candidates field; + Commitment to teaching, research, and program service. **COMPENSATION** **Salary Range:** $79,630 - $94,017 CUNY offers faculty a competitive compensation and benefits package covering health insurance, pension and retirement benefits, paid parental leave, and savings programs. We also provide mentoring and support for research, scholarship, and publication as part of our commitment to ongoing faculty professional development. **HOW TO APPLY** **Only applications submitted through CUNYfirst will be considered for this position.** If you are viewing this job posting in CUNYfirst, please click on \"Apply Now\" on the bottom of this page and follow the instructions. If you are viewing this job posting externally, please apply as follows: + Go to https://cuny.jobs/ + Search for Job Opening ID number **32849** + Click on the \"Apply Now\" button and follow the instructions. Applications, including the following must be uploaded to the CUNYfirst job application website as a single PDF document: + Curriculum Vitae; + Cover Letter; + 1-2-page teaching statement; + Names and contact information for three (3) professional academic references who may be contacted to provide letters of recommendation. **CLOSING DATE** November 25, 2026 **JOB SEARCH CATEGORY** CUNY Job Posting: Faculty **EQUAL EMPLOYMENT OPPORTUNITY** CUNY encourages people with disabilities, minorities, veterans and women to apply. At CUNY, Italian Americans are also included among our protected groups. Applicants and employees will not be discriminated against on the basis of any legally protected category, including sexual orientation or gender identity. EEO/AA/Vet/Disability Employer. Job ID: 32849 Location: City College of New York Job Type: Full-Time"
    },
    "https://cuny.jobs/CA013A250558461D97F1F47DCE8EE18925": {
      "title": "Assistant or Associate Professor of Earth & Atmospheric Sciences",
      "description": "Assistant or Associate Professor of Earth & Atmospheric Sciences. Assistant or Associate Professor of Earth & Atmospheric Sciences **FACULTY VACANCY ANNOUNCEMENT** **Position Details** The Department of Earth and Atmospheric Sciences (EAS) at The City College of New York (CCNY) invites applications for a full-time, tenure-track Assistant or Associate Professor in Atmospheric Science. The search targets atmospheric scientists with expertise in data assimilation, numerical modeling, and related data-intensive approaches to weather, air quality, and urban environmental hazards. Preference will be given to candidates with experience applying AI and machine-learning tools in these areas. The successful candidate will help build a research group focused on urban weather and climate and collaborate across disciplines to advance urban resilience. The candidate is expected to establish a vigorous, externally funded research program that broadens and complements existing departmental strengths, develop new courses, and teach existing courses in atmospheric science. CCNY is the flagship campus of the City University of New York (CUNY), the nations largest urban public university system. EAS offers B.S. and M.S. degrees and co-grants Ph.D. degrees with the CUNY Graduate Center. CCNY serves a broad range of students and has an exemplary record as an engine of social and economic mobility. The EAS Department seeks candidates who appreciate and support the Colleges mission in this regard. Candidate must be legally authorized to work Full-time in the United States. **QUALIFICATIONS** Ph.D. in Atmospheric Science (or a closely related field coupled with publications focused on atmospheric science or urban environmental hazards). Also required are the ability to teach successfully, demonstrated scholarly achievement, and ability to cooperate with others for the good of the institution. **Preferred Qualifications** + Ability to teach effectively across a wide range of learning styles and levels, from undergraduates to doctoral students. + Evidence of scholarly achievement and the ability to work collaboratively for the good of the institution. + Demonstrate the potential to maintain an extramurally funded research program. **COMPENSATION** **Assistant Professor:** $105,588 - $114,091 **Associate Professor:** $117,971 - $134,808 CUNY offers faculty a competitive compensation and benefits package covering health insurance, pension and retirement benefits, paid parental leave, and savings programs. We also provide mentoring and support for research, scholarship, and publication as part of our commitment to ongoing faculty professional development. **HOW TO APPLY** **Only applications submitted through CUNYfirst will be considered for this position.** If you are viewing this job posting in CUNYfirst, please click on \"Apply Now\" on the bottom of this page and follow the instructions. If you are viewing this job posting externally, please apply as follows: + Go to https://cuny.jobs/ + Search for Job Opening ID number **32897** + Click on the \"Apply Now\" button and follow the instructions. Applications, including the following must be uploaded to the CUNYfirst job application website as a single PDF document: + Cover Letter + Curriculum Vitae including Publication List + Research Statement (maximum 2 pages) + Teaching Statement (maximum 2 pages) + Names and contact information for three professional references who could provide Letters of Reference if we request them (do not send letters with the application). **CLOSING DATE** December 9, 2026 **JOB SEARCH CATEGORY** CUNY Job Posting: Faculty **EQUAL EMPLOYMENT OPPORTUNITY** CUNY encourages people with disabilities, minorities, veterans and women to apply. At CUNY, Italian Americans are also included among our protected groups. Applicants and employees will not be discriminated against on the basis of any legally protected category, including sexual orientation or gender identity. EEO/AA/Vet/Disability Employer. Job ID: 32897 Location: City College of New York"
    },
    "https://cuny.jobs/7155DFAEA8BE4CBABB1711F9AE1BD29625": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor - Computational Neuroscience - Psychology (Tenure-Track)",
      "description": "Assistant Professor - Computational Neuroscience - Psychology (Tenure-Track). Assistant Professor - Computational Neuroscience - Psychology (Tenure-Track) **FACULTY VACANCY ANNOUNCEMENT** The Department of Psychology at the City College of New York (CCNY) invites applications for a full-time, tenure-track faculty position in computational neuroscience at the rank of Assistant Professor to begin in Fall 2027. The CCNY Psychology Department is a vibrant, diverse department located in Harlem, serving over 2000 undergraduate majors, with masters programs in General Psychology and Mental Health Counseling. The department offers a doctoral program in Clinical Psychology; faculty also participate in Ph.D. programs in Neuroscience and Psychology at the CUNY Graduate Center, and the M.S. program in Cognitive Neuroscience. Specialty is open, but we are particularly interested in individuals with a robust research program in cognitive and/or affective neuroscience. We seek expertise in neurophysiological recording (fMRI, fNIRS, M/EEG, PET, peripheral physiology) and in computational analysis methods including machine learning (e.g., multi-voxel pattern classification analysis, SVMs, decoders). With this hire, we seek to bridge between the Psychology Departments existing strengths in clinical and translational work and our developing cluster in computational neuroscience. The department maintains strong affiliations with the Center for Discovery and Innovation (CDI), a state-of-the-art research facility designed to foster interdisciplinary research, with imaging core and behavioral testing space shared with its twin building, the CUNY Advanced Science Research Center (ASRC), which manages a dedicated neuroscience initiative that includes a 3T MRI scanner on campus. The CUNY Graduate Center also hosts the CUNY Initiative for Theoretical Sciences (ITS), a collaborative research hub that emphasizes mathematical and theoretical approaches to the natural sciences. Computational resources include the CUNY High Performance Computing Center (CUNY-HPCC), and a recent $75 million gift from the Simons Foundation, which will expand computational expertise and capacity across CUNY and establish a M.S. program in Computational Science. Opportunities for research collaborations exist at the Grove School of Engineering, CUNY School of Medicine and the U54 partnership between CCNY and the Memorial Sloan Kettering Cancer Center, plus additional intramural programs. Successful applicants are expected to maintain an active program of research, obtain external grant support, publish theoretical and empirical research in top-tier journals, be committed to both undergraduate and graduate education, and provide mentoring to students. The position will include teaching, research, mentorship, and departmental service, including administrative, supervisory, and other functions. **QUALIFICATIONS** A Ph.D. in psychology, neuroscience, applied mathematics, computer science, engineering, or a similarly allied field is required. Also required are the ability to teach successfully, demonstrated scholarly achievement, and ability to cooperate with others for the good of the institution. **Preferred Qualifications** Experience in providing mentoring/advisement to neuroscience students. The candidate is expected to maintain an active program of research, publish theoretical and empirical research in top-tier journals, be committed to both undergraduate and graduate education, and provide mentoring/advisement to students. Potential for obtaining external grant support. **COMPENSATION** **Assistant Professor:** $79,630 - $94,017 CUNY offers faculty a competitive compensation and benefits package covering health insurance, pension and retirement benefits, paid parental leave, and savings programs. We also provide mentoring and support for research, scholarship, and publication as part of our commitment to ongoing faculty professional development. **HOW TO APPLY** **Only applications submitted through CUNYfirst will be considered for this position.** If you are viewing this job posting in CUNYfirst, please click on \"Apply Now\" on the bottom of this page and follow the instructions. If you are viewing this job posting externally, please apply as follows: + Go to https://cuny.jobs/ + Search for Job Opening ID number **32920** + Click on the \"Apply Now\" button and follow the instructions. Applications, including the following must be uploaded to the CUNYfirst job application website as a single PDF document: 1. Cover Letter 2. Curriculum Vitae 3. Statement of teaching interests and goals 4. Teaching and peer classroom evaluations 5. Names and contact information for three professional references **CLOSING DATE** December 7, 2026 **JOB SEARCH CATEGORY** CUNY Job Posting: Faculty **EQUAL EMPLOYMENT OPPORTUNITY** CUNY encourages people with disabilities, minorities, veterans and women to apply. At CUNY, Italian Americans are also included among our protected groups. Applicants and employees will not be discriminated against on the basis of any legally protected category, including sexual orientation or gender identity. EEO/AA/Vet/Disability Employer. Job ID: 32920 Location: City College of New York Job Type: Full-Time"
    },
    "https://cuny.jobs/3AA0219BBF4C454DA630FCA09496062925": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor â€“ Substance Use - Psychology (Tenure-Track)",
      "description": "Assistant Professor â€“ Substance Use - Psychology (Tenure-Track). Assistant Professor Substance Use - Psychology (Tenure-Track) **FACULTY VACANCY ANNOUNCEMENT** The Department of Psychology at the City College of New York (CCNY) invites applications for a full-time, tenure-track faculty position in substance use at the rank of Assistant Professor to begin in Fall 2027. The CCNY Psychology Department is a vibrant, diverse department located in Harlem, serving over 2000 undergraduate majors, with masters programs in General Psychology and Mental Health Counseling. Over 200 students participate in our Credentialed Alcoholism and Substance Abuse Counselor (CASAC) program, which provides the educational and practicum hours required for certification as a substance use counselor by the New York State Office of Addiction Services and Supports (OASAS). The department offers an APA-accredited doctoral program in Clinical Psychology; faculty also participate in Ph.D. programs in Neuroscience and Psychology at the CUNY Graduate Center, and the M.S. program in Cognitive Neuroscience. We seek a scientist investigating basic or translational mechanisms underpinning substance use and/or the treatment of substance use and misuse. The specific area of research is open, but can include neuroimaging, therapeutics, technology-based interventions, translational neuroscience, and implementation science. With this hire, we seek to build on the Psychology Departments existing strengths in clinical and translational research in substance use. The department maintains strong affiliations with the Center for Discovery and Innovation (CDI), a state-of-the-art research facility designed to foster interdisciplinary research, with imaging core and behavioral testing space shared with its twin building, the CUNY Advanced Science Research Center (ASRC), which manages a dedicated neuroscience initiative that includes a 3T MRI scanner on campus. Opportunities for research collaborations exist at the Grove School of Engineering, CUNY School of Medicine and the U54 partnership between CCNY and the Memorial Sloan Kettering Cancer Center, plus additional intramural programs. Successful applicants are expected to maintain an active program of research, obtain external grant support, publish theoretical and empirical research in top-tier journals, be committed to both undergraduate and graduate education, and provide mentoring to students. The position will include teaching, research, mentorship, and departmental service, including administrative, supervisory, and other functions. **QUALIFICATIONS** A Ph.D. in psychology, neuroscience, clinical psychology, public health, or a similarly allied field is required. Also required are the ability to teach successfully, demonstrated scholarship or achievement, and ability to cooperate with others for the good of the institution. **Preferred Qualifications** Experience in providing mentoring/advisement to neuroscience students. The candidate is expected to maintain an active program of research, publish theoretical and empirical research in top-tier journals, be committed to both undergraduate and graduate education, and provide mentoring/advisement to students. Potential for obtaining external grant support. **COMPENSATION** **Assistant Professor:** $79,630 - $94,017 CUNY offers faculty a competitive compensation and benefits package covering health insurance, pension and retirement benefits, paid parental leave, and savings programs. We also provide mentoring and support for research, scholarship, and publication as part of our commitment to ongoing faculty professional development. **HOW TO APPLY** **Only applications submitted through CUNYfirst will be considered for this position.** If you are viewing this job posting in CUNYfirst, please click on \"Apply Now\" on the bottom of this page and follow the instructions. If you are viewing this job posting externally, please apply as follows: + Go to https://cuny.jobs/ + Search for Job Opening ID number **32918** + Click on the \"Apply Now\" button and follow the instructions. Applications, including the following must be uploaded to the CUNYfirst job application website as a single PDF document: 1. Cover Letter 2. Curriculum Vitae 3. Statement of teaching interests and goals 4. Teaching and peer classroom evaluations 5. Names and contact information for three professional references **CLOSING DATE** December 7, 2026 **JOB SEARCH CATEGORY** CUNY Job Posting: Faculty **EQUAL EMPLOYMENT OPPORTUNITY** CUNY encourages people with disabilities, minorities, veterans and women to apply. At CUNY, Italian Americans are also included among our protected groups. Applicants and employees will not be discriminated against on the basis of any legally protected category, including sexual orientation or gender identity. EEO/AA/Vet/Disability Employer. Job ID: 32918 Location: City College of New York Job Type: Full-Time"
    },
    "https://cuny.jobs/30F17DD9D424441AAEA348AD4AD497CB25": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor (Tenure track) - Computer Science",
      "description": "Assistant Professor (Tenure track) - Computer Science. Assistant Professor (Tenure track) - Computer Science **FACULTY VACANCY ANNOUNCEMENT** **Position Details** The Computer Science Department at City College of New York is inviting applications for two tenure-track positions at the Assistant Professor level. Each selected candidate will lead a variety of teaching and research activities in computer science, collaborating with faculty members. Applicants in all areas of computer science will be considered. Areas of particular interest include theory and software fundamentals, data science, artificial intelligence and machine learning, cybersecurity, computer architecture, programmable and adaptive systems, and parallel and distributed computing. Successful candidates will be expected to establish and sustain an independent research program, teach both undergraduate and graduate courses, mentor students at all levels, and contribute to the overall academic community within the department. The recruitment committee will evaluate all demonstrative and evidential achievements that candidates can provide to meet these academic missions. As a department within the Grove School of Engineering, the City College Computer Science Department undergraduate and graduate curriculum and research activities of its faculty reflect synergies with engineering fields that differentiate its programs within the City University system. For information about the Grove School of Engineering and the Computer Science Department please see https://www.ccny.cuny.edu/engineering. **QUALIFICATIONS** Ph.D. in Computer Science or related field. Also required are the ability to teach successfully, demonstrated scholarly achievement, and ability to cooperate with others for the good of the institution. **Preferred Qualifications** The successful candidates must demonstrate a strong commitment to excellence in undergraduate and graduate teaching and the ability to present strong and sustainable research programs with credible prospects for external support appropriate to their fields. **COMPENSATION** **Assistant Professor:** $94,017 - $114,091 Salaries above the contractual base are available commensurate with experience and PSC-CUNY contract. CUNY offers faculty a competitive compensation and benefits package covering health insurance, pension and retirement benefits, paid parental leave, and savings programs. We also provide mentoring and support for research, scholarship, and publication as part of our commitment to ongoing faculty professional development. **HOW TO APPLY** **Only applications submitted through CUNYfirst will be considered for this position.** If you are viewing this job posting in CUNYfirst, please click on \"Apply Now\" on the bottom of this page and follow the instructions. If you are viewing this job posting externally, please apply as follows: + Go to https://cuny.jobs/ + Search for Job Opening ID number **32906** + Click on the \"Apply Now\" button and follow the instructions. Applications, including the following must be uploaded to the CUNYfirst job application website as a single PDF document: 1. Cover Letter 2. Curriculum Vitae 3. Teaching statement that details your classroom philosophy, methods, and concrete evidence of your effectiveness, including past experience and if possible, a few student reviews of your teaching 4. Well-structured research statement that showcase your scholarly identity, past accomplishments, and future plans. 5. Names and contact information for four professional references, who may be contacted to provide letters of recommendation. **CLOSING DATE** December 7, 2026 **JOB SEARCH CATEGORY** CUNY Job Posting: Faculty **EQUAL EMPLOYMENT OPPORTUNITY** CUNY encourages people with disabilities, minorities, veterans and women to apply. At CUNY, Italian Americans are also included among our protected groups. Applicants and employees will not be discriminated against on the basis of any legally protected category, including sexual orientation or gender identity. EEO/AA/Vet/Disability Employer. Job ID: 32906 Location: City College of New York Job Type: Full-Time"
    },
    "https://cuny.jobs/8F700AC821F64252869ECFB01815AFB225": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor- Civil Engineering (Tenure-track)",
      "description": "Assistant Professor- Civil Engineering (Tenure-track). Assistant Professor- Civil Engineering (Tenure-track) **FACULTY VACANCY ANNOUNCEMENT** The Department of Civil Engineering at the City College of New York (CCNY) of the City University of New York (CUNY) is seeking candidates for an Assistant Professor, tenure-track faculty member with expertise in Geotechnical Engineering and Geomechanics. The successful candidate will be expected to develop an externally funded research program that complements existing departmental expertise. Specific geotechnical engineering and geomechanics sub-areas of interest to the department include: Soil-structure interaction; Geo-hazard analysis and mitigation; Geophysical mapping and analysis of geohazards, such as sinkholes, landslides, etc.; Application of multi-scale computational modeling, data science, machine learning, and artificial intelligence techniques to geotechnical engineering; durability and life-cycle assessment of geotechnical infrastructure; and performance-based design and adaptation of geotechnical assets for infrastructure resilience. The Department of Civil Engineering has 17 full-time faculty members in four areas of concentration: Environmental Engineering, Structures/Mechanics, Transportation, and Water Resources. Our faculty engage in a wide variety of research efforts with international, national, regional, and local agencies as well as private entities to address both regional and global complex infrastructure demands and challenges. The Department houses several CCNY centers and CUNY institutes, including the CUNY Environmental Crossroads Initiative, the Center for Water Resources and Environmental Research, the Institute for Municipal Waste Research, the Institute for Transportation Systems, NOAA Center for Earth Systems Sciences and Remote Sensing Technologies, and the University Transportation Research Center. CCNYs Grove School of Engineerings $35 million in external research last year classified it in the top 10% of US engineering institutions. CUNY is the largest public urban university system in the United States. CCNY is the founding institution of CUNY and one of CUNYs flagship campuses, is a minority serving institution, and is the only public institution offering bachelors, masters and doctoral degrees in civil engineering in New York City. The Department of Civil Engineering is committed to the rigorous training of students from diverse socioeconomic backgrounds to become future leaders in engineering firms, agencies, and professional organizations. **QUALIFICATIONS** Ph.D. in Civil Engineering.Also required are the ability to teach successfully, demonstrated scholarly achievement, and ability to cooperate with others for the good of the institution. **Preferred Qualifications** A strong research background in geotechnical engineering and geomechanics, geohazards, and be capable of teaching fundamental undergraduate and advanced graduate courses in civil engineering. **COMPENSATION** **Salary Range:** $94,017 - $114,091 Salaries above the contractual base may be offered, commensurate with experience and PSC-CUNY contract. CUNY offers faculty a competitive compensation and benefits package covering health insurance, pension and retirement benefits, paid parental leave, and savings programs. We also provide mentoring and support for research, scholarship, and publication as part of our commitment to ongoing faculty professional development. **HOW TO APPLY** **Only applications submitted through CUNYfirst will be considered for this position.** If you are viewing this job posting in CUNYfirst, please click on \"Apply Now\" on the bottom of this page and follow the instructions. If you are viewing this job posting externally, please apply as follows: + Go to https://cuny.jobs/ + Search for Job Opening ID number TBD + Click on the \"Apply Now\" button and follow the instructions. Applications, including the following must be uploaded to the CUNYfirst job application website as a single PDF document: + Cover Letter; + Research Statement; + Statement of Teaching Philosophy; + Curriculum Vitae **CLOSING DATE** December 14, 2026 **JOB SEARCH CATEGORY** CUNY Job Posting: Faculty **EQUAL EMPLOYMENT OPPORTUNITY** CUNY encourages people with disabilities, minorities, veterans and women to apply. At CUNY, Italian Americans are also included among our protected groups. Applicants and employees will not be discriminated against on the basis of any legally protected category, including sexual orientation or gender identity. EEO/AA/Vet/Disability Employer. Job ID: 32966 Location: City College of New York Job Type: Full-Time"
    },
    "https://cuny.jobs/C52A1E63390941DCA95E8D45577B7C7025": {
      "title": "Lecturer (Doctoral Schedule) â€“ Civil Engineering",
      "description": "Lecturer (Doctoral Schedule) â€“ Civil Engineering. Lecturer (Doctoral Schedule) Civil Engineering **FACULTY VACANCY ANNOUNCEMENT** The Civil Engineering Department at The City College of the City University of New York invites applications to fill a teaching position at the rank of Lecturer (Doctoral Schedule) in areas related to civil engineering design and practice. The successful candidate should be prepared to teach in the undergraduate and graduate programs, develop innovative instructional methodologies and participate in curriculum development efforts in collaboration with other faculty members. The applicants will be expected to have teaching experience in courses related to but not limited to computer aided design (CAD), engineering materials and associated labs, engineering and construction management, and senior (capstone) design. Familiarity in applying artificial intelligence (AI) and machine learning (ML) to instruction will be favorably considered. For information about the Grove School of Engineering and the Civil Engineering Department please see https://www.ccny.cuny.edu/engineering. **QUALIFICATIONS** PhD in Civil Engineering from an accredited university. The candidate is also required to have the ability to teach successfully, demonstrated scholarly achievement, and ability to cooperate with others for the good of the institution. **Preferred Qualifications** Experience in teaching at the undergraduate and graduate level and a strong commitment to excellence in teaching courses related to but not limited to computer aided design (CAD), engineering materials and associated labs, engineering and construction management, and senior capstone design. A desirable candidate is also expected to have a profession engineering licensure in civil engineering in the United States or should have the ability to receive the licensure within one year of appointment. Candidates with professional experience in civil engineering profession and familiarity with AI will be preferred. **COMPENSATION** **Salary Range:** $93,080 - $111,455 CUNY offers faculty a competitive compensation and benefits package covering health insurance, pension and retirement benefits, paid parental leave, and savings programs. We also provide mentoring and support for research, scholarship, and publication as part of our commitment to ongoing faculty professional development. **HOW TO APPLY** **Only applications submitted through CUNYfirst will be considered for this position.** If you are viewing this job posting in CUNYfirst, please click on \"Apply Now\" on the bottom of this page and follow the instructions. If you are viewing this job posting externally, please apply as follows: + Go to https://cuny.jobs/ + Search for Job Opening ID number **32956** + Click on the \"Apply Now\" button and follow the instructions. Applications, including the following must be uploaded to the CUNYfirst job application website as a single PDF document: + Curriculum Vitae; + Cover Letter; + Teaching Philosophy Statement **CLOSING DATE** November 14, 2026 **JOB SEARCH CATEGORY** CUNY Job Posting: Faculty **EQUAL EMPLOYMENT OPPORTUNITY** CUNY encourages people with disabilities, minorities, veterans and women to apply. At CUNY, Italian Americans are also included among our protected groups. Applicants and employees will not be discriminated against on the basis of any legally protected category, including sexual orientation or gender identity. EEO/AA/Vet/Disability Employer. Job ID: 32956 Location: City College of New York"
    },
    "https://cuny.jobs/ECBFB96692CF408193C0FC2D5DE81ABD25": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Biology (Tenure-Track) - Comparative or Quantitative Genomics",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Biology (Tenure-Track) - Comparative or Quantitative Genomics. Assistant Professor of Biology (Tenure-Track) - Comparative or Quantitative Genomics **FACULTY VACANCY ANNOUNCEMENT** The Department of Biology at The City College of New York (CCNY) seeks a full-time tenure-track Assistant Professor whose research program incorporates Comparative or Quantitative Genomics. The successful candidate will develop a productive research program in genomics, broadly defined, bridging biological disciplines and working across levels of biological systems. The candidate should use cutting edge genomic approaches to address fundamental questions in biology, which may include immunology, bacterial pathogenesis, neurobiology, developmental biology, disease ecology, or evolutionary biology across any system including non-model organisms (animals, plants, or microbes). Preference will be given to candidates who develop modern genomic tools and statistical approaches that leverage artificial intelligence/machine learning to investigate the forces shaping biological diversity, evolutionary change, or gene interaction networks. We are particularly interested in candidates with expertise in the following areas: + functional and regulatory genomics; + comparative phylogenomics to reconstruct developmental and/or evolutionary histories; + genome structure, such as structural variation or gene regulation; + population genomics and conservation genetics; and/or + genomic variation underlying adaptation, complex traits, or speciation. CCNY is the flagship campus of the City University of New York (CUNY), a public university system in New York City. CCNY offers B.S. and M.S. degrees in Biology and co-grants Ph.D.s along with the CUNY Graduate Center. CCNY serves a broad range of students and has an exemplary record as an engine of social and economic mobility. The candidates research should be conducive to interactions with one or more of the Departments research strengths in ecology/evolution, neuroscience, and molecular/cellular biology. Research that bridges these areas would be particularly appealing to the hiring committee. The candidates research program at CCNY will be integrated into their teaching, mentoring, and advising of undergraduate and graduate students. They will contribute to the Department via service on departmental, college or university committees, and teaching in undergraduate or graduate courses. Candidates must be legally authorized to work full-time in the United States. **QUALIFICATIONS** Ph.D. in Biology or related field is required. **Preferred Qualifications:** + Postdoctoral research experience in a relevant field. + Demonstrated the potential to maintain an extramurally funded research program. + Prior experience mentoring student researchers. + Ability to contribute to service and teaching roles. **COMPENSATION** Salary Range: $90,838 - $102,017 CUNY offers faculty a competitive compensation and benefits package covering health insurance, pension and retirement benefits, paid parental leave, and savings programs. We also provide mentoring and support for research, scholarship, and publication as part of our commitment to ongoing faculty professional development. **HOW TO APPLY** ****Only applications submitted through CUNYfirst will be considered for this position.**** If you are viewing this job posting in CUNYfirst, please click on \"Apply Now\" on the bottom of this page and follow the instructions. If you are viewing this job posting externally, please apply as follows: + Go to https://cuny.jobs/ + Search for Job Opening ID number: **31165** + Click on the \"Apply Now\" button and follow the instructions. Applications, including the following must be uploaded to the CUNYfirst job application website as a single PDF document: + Cover Letter + Curriculum Vitae including Publication List + Research Statement (maximum 2 pages) + Teaching Statement (maximum 2 pages) + Names and contact information of three professional references who could provide Letters of Reference **CLOSING DATE** Open until filled with review of applications to begin on December 21, 2025. **JOB SEARCH CATEGORY** CUNY Job Posting: Faculty **EQUAL EMPLOYMENT OPPORTUNITY** CUNY encourages people with disabilities, minorities, veterans and women to apply. At CUNY, Italian Americans are also included among our protected groups. Applicants and employees will not be discriminated against on the basis of any legally protected category, including sexual orientation or gender identity. EEO/AA/Vet/Disability Employer. Job ID: 31165 Location: City College of New York Job Type: Full-Time"
    },
    "https://cuny.jobs/F7AF640E15CE4F1DAC50D2FF48E0F93A25": {
      "title": "Associate or Full Professor â€“ Computational Materials Science",
      "description": "Associate or Full Professor â€“ Computational Materials Science. Associate or Full Professor Computational Materials Science **FACULTY VACANCY ANNOUNCEMENT** The City College of New York is pleased to announce a faculty search as part of the CUNY Martin S. Spergel Initiative supported by the Simons Foundation. The CCNY Department of Chemistry and Biochemistry seeks a mid- to senior-career hire (Associate/Full Professor) in Computational Chemistry/Materials Science, or closely related field. The successful candidate will join the CUNY AI-Biome Initiative, a new multi-campus research effort building on research strengths in bio-inspired materials, sustainable chemistry, nanotechnology and computational chemistry, with a focus on integrating artificial intelligence, and autonomous experimentation to accelerate data-driven molecular materials discovery and sustainable manufacturing. The appointee will coordinate computational efforts within the AI-Biome cluster and play a leadership role in shaping this interdisciplinary research community. Desired areas of expertise may include, but are not limited to: + Familiarity with universal descriptors for structure/reactivity of surfaces and interfaces. + Development of accurate, efficient, and transferable many-body potentials. + AI-accelerated modeling of strongly correlated electronic systems. + Predictive modeling for self-organizing and bioinspired materials. + Autonomous and AI-guided experimental workflows for materials discovery. The successful candidate will collaborate with experimental groups across CUNY, including the Advanced Science Research Center (ASRC) build partnerships and work with external organizations, including Empire AI. In addition to developing an independent research program, the selected faculty member will play a key role in recruiting and mentoring three new Assistant Professors in complementary areas of materials science, AI-driven scientific discovery, and sustainable design, helping to shape the long-term vision of the AI-Biome initiative. The position aligns with CCNYs strengths in sustainable energy materials, catalysis, molecular assemblies, and quantum materials, and leverages CUNYs collaborative network, including CUNYs Advanced Science Research Center, the ASRC CAT program that supports industry partnerships, the newly established Empire AI program, and highly active external funding programs (including PhD training) supported by Federal agencies such NSF, NIH, DoD, and DoE, and major foundations. Applicants for this position would demonstrate an outstanding record of research accomplishments, strong teaching credentials, and established leadership in their field. The successful candidate will be expected to contribute to the advancement of the department and the College by sustaining a robust, externally funded research program; engaging actively in undergraduate and graduate teaching and mentoring; and fostering multidisciplinary research and educational collaborations across CCNY and the broader CUNY community. **QUALIFICATIONS** **For Associate or Full Professor:** Ph.D. (or foreign equivalent) in computational chemistry/materials science, or closely related field. **Preferred Qualifications** + Strong record of research performance in computational materials science. + Established leadership in the field, with evidence of interdisciplinary activities. + Demonstrated ability to secure external funding and manage a research group. + Significant postdoctoral and managerial research experience in the relevant field. + Experience mentoring student researchers and university level teaching. + Expertise applying AI, machine learning, or quantum methods to materials design. + Background in modeling complex molecular systems, self-assembly, or surfaces/interfaces. + Candidates must be legally authorized to work full-time in the United States. **COMPENSATION** **Associate Professor:** $121,194 - $129,041 **Full Professor:** $122,201 - $155,388 CUNY offers faculty a competitive compensation and benefits package covering health insurance, pension and retirement benefits, paid parental leave, and savings programs. We also provide mentoring and support for research, scholarship, and publication as part of our commitment to ongoing faculty professional development. **HOW TO APPLY** Only applications submitted through CUNYfirst will be considered for this position. If you are viewing this job posting in CUNYfirst, please click on \"Apply Now\" at the bottom of this page and follow the instructions. If you are viewing this job posting externally, please apply as follows: + Go to https://cuny.jobs/ + Search for Job Opening ID number **31368** + Click on the Apply Now button and follow the instructions Applications must include the following as a single PDF document: 1. Cover Letter 2. Curriculum Vitae including Publication List 3. Research Statement (maximum 2 pages) 4. Teaching Statement (maximum 2 pages) 5. Mentoring Statement (maximum 2 pages) 6. Names and contact information of three persons who could provide Letters of Reference **CLOSING DATE** Open until filled with the review of applications to begin on January 17, 2026. **JOB SEARCH CATEGORY** CUNY Job Posting: Faculty **EQUAL EMPLOYMENT OPPORTUNITY** CUNY encourages people with disabilities, minorities, veterans and women to apply. At CUNY, Italian Americans are also included among our protected groups. Applicants and employees will not be discriminated against on the basis of any legally protected category, including sexual orientation or gender identity. EEO/AA/Vet/Disability Employer. Job ID: 31368 Location: City College of New York Job Type: Full-Time"
    },
    "https://cuny.jobs/BA291038E0B74CF89C2E494ACCBFF1A225": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of American Politics",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of American Politics. Assistant Professor of American Politics **FACULTY VACANCY ANNOUNCEMENT** The Department of Political Science at the City College of New York (CCNY) invites applications for a tenure-track assistant professorship in American Politics to begin in the fall of 2027. The substantive specialization and methodological approach for this position are open. Candidates must be willing and able to teach a large multi-section Introduction to American Government course once a year. Candidates should also have a strong commitment to City College's historic mission of providing a public first-rate education to all students, regardless of means or background. Successful candidates will be required to teach two courses per semester until tenure review, pursue an active research agenda, participate in the life of the department and the college, and have completed all requirements for a Ph.D. by the start date. **QUALIFICATIONS** Required Qualifications + Ph.D. degree in Political Science or a related field from an accredited institution. + Demonstrated potential for quality scholarship. + The ability to teach successfully and the ability to cooperate with others for the good of the institution. **COMPENSATION** **Salary Range:** $82,384 - $94,017 CUNY offers faculty a competitive compensation and benefits package covering health insurance, pension and retirement benefits, paid parental leave, and savings programs. We also provide mentoring and support for research, scholarship, and publication as part of our commitment to ongoing faculty professional development. **HOW TO APPLY** **Only applications submitted through CUNYfirst will be considered for this position.** If you are viewing this job posting in CUNYfirst, please click on \"Apply Now\" at the bottom of this page and follow the instructions. If you are viewing this job posting externally, please apply as follows: + Go to https://cuny.jobs/ + Search for Job Opening ID number: **31735** + Click on the \"Apply Now\" button and follow the instructions. Applications, including the following must be uploaded to the CUNYfirst job application website as a single PDF document: 1. Cover Letter 2. Curriculum Vitae 3. Writing Sample (no more than 40 pages) 4. Names and contact information for three professional references **CLOSING DATE** Open Until Filled with the review of applications to begin on September 15, 2026 **JOB SEARCH CATEGORY** CUNY Job Posting: Faculty **EQUAL EMPLOYMENT OPPORTUNITY** CUNY encourages people with disabilities, minorities, veterans and women to apply. At CUNY, Italian Americans are also included among our protected groups. Applicants and employees will not be discriminated against on the basis of any legally protected category, including sexual orientation or gender identity. EEO/AA/Vet/Disability Employer. Job ID: 31735 Location: City College of New York Job Type: Full-Time"
    },
    "https://cuny.jobs/8E78D1A867754581956F450C15ECA05C25": {
      "title": "Lecturer - Urban Sustainability",
      "description": "Lecturer - Urban Sustainability. Lecturer - Urban Sustainability **FACULTY VACANCY ANNOUNCEMENT** Since its founding in 1847, The City College of New York (CCNY) has been true to its legacy of access, opportunity, and transformation. To respond to the increasing demand for a workforce equipped to meet the sustainability challenges of the 21st century, The City College of New York offers the Sustainability in the Urban Environment graduate program, offering a Master of Science Degree and an Advanced Certificate in Urban Sustainability. This innovative, hybrid, and interdisciplinary program draws upon emerging approaches in the disciplines of architecture, engineering, science, and social sciences. The program reports directly to the CCNY Provosts Office, and academic offerings draw across four CCNY divisions and schools: The Division of Science, The Grove School of Engineering, The Spitzer School of Architecture, and The Colin Powell School for Civic and Global Leadership. The Sustainability in the Urban Environment Program seeks a full-time Lecturer to instruct core and elective courses in the Program and mentor capstone research projects. The Lecturer will report directly to the Sustainability Programs Director. The Lecturer will have primary responsibility for teaching and for research project oversight with a focus on Life-Cycle Assessment. The lecturer may contribute to the overall academic development of graduate students. The lecturer position has an annual 24-hour teaching credit workload per 9-month academic year. They will teach three courses per semester with additional administrative reassigned time for oversight of multiple capstone projects. Additional Program and school service work is required. Lecturers receive a certificate of continuous employment (contractual tenure) upon their sixth-year reappointment. The appointment will start in the Fall 2026 semester. Given the 9-month teaching appointment, Lecturers enjoy summers off to engage in scholarly activities such as research and paid adjunct teaching, and personal time off. Duties include but are not limited to: Teaching + Deliver one core course of the Program - Industrial Ecology and Life Cycle Analysis - every semester. Each academic year, this 3-credit hour course is offered once online and once in person, both in the evening. + Deliver one to three elective courses per semester with an emphasis on sustainability topics in engineering. The courses are offered in a hyflex modality in the evening. Hyflex courses include some students completing electives in person and others online. Some courses may include: Advanced Life Cycle-Assessment, Sustainable Materials, Topics in Renewable Energy, and Topics in Energy Transition. New elective course development is welcome with oversight and approval of the Sustainability Executive Committee. + Propose at least three capstone research projects per semester in relevant areas of expertise. These can be based on personal research interests or partner with outside organizations. Interdisciplinary student teams of 2 to 4 students select projects from a portfolio of CCNY faculty offerings. Depending on how many projects move forward each semester, the Lecturer will be tasked with mentoring one to three capstone teams concurrently. + Assess and evaluate student performance, provide feedback, and support student academic progress. Programmatic responsibilities + General academic advising for Sustainability students as needed. + Participate as an active member in the Campus Sustainability Council and support greening the campus when or where relevant. + Assist in CCNY Urban Sustainability events, communications, and commencements. + Assist with other administrative tasks as needed. + Contribute to the Programs student recruitment and orientation. **QUALIFICATIONS** + A Masters degree in a relevant field such as Sustainability, Environmental Engineering, Environmental Management, Energy Engineering, or Environmental Science. + Demonstrated teaching experience, both in person and online. + Proficiency in Microsoft 365 and Course Management Systems such as Brightspace. **Preferred Qualifications** + A PhD in a relevant field by the start of employment, such as Sustainability, Environmental Engineering, Environmental Management, Energy Engineering, or Environmental Science. + Commitment to promoting diversity, equity, and inclusion and demonstrated experience teaching a diverse student body. + Knowledge of current educational technologies and their integration into teaching, especially in a hyflex capacity. + Experience in curriculum development and assessment. **COMPENSATION** **Salary Range:** $84,202 - $95,302 commensurate with qualifications, experience, and education. CUNY offers faculty a competitive compensation and benefits package covering health insurance, pension and retirement benefits, paid parental leave, and savings programs. We also provide mentoring and support for research, scholarship, and publication as part of our commitment to ongoing faculty professional development. **HOW TO APPLY** Only applications submitted through CUNYfirst will be considered for this position. If you are viewing this job posting in CUNYfirst, please click on \"Apply Now\" at the bottom of this page and follow the instructions. If you are viewing this job posting externally, please apply as follows: + Go to https://cuny.jobs/ + Search for Job Opening ID number: 31836 + Click on the \"Apply Now\" button and follow the instructions. Applications, including the following must be uploaded to the CUNYfirst job application website as a single PDF document: 1. Letter of interest / Cover Letter (including description of teaching experience and philosophy) 2. Curriculum Vitae / Resume 3. Names and contact information for two professional references **CLOSING DATE** Open until filled with review of resumes to begin on March 21, 2026. **JOB SEARCH CATEGORY** CUNY Job Posting: Faculty **EQUAL EMPLOYMENT OPPORTUNITY** CUNY encourages people with disabilities, minorities, veterans and women to apply. At CUNY, Italian Americans are also included among our protected groups. Applicants and employees will not be discriminated against on the basis of any legally protected category, including sexual orientation or gender identity. EEO/AA/Vet/Disability Employer. Job ID: 31836 Location: City College of New York Job Type: Full-Time"
    },
    "https://cuny.jobs/E71E5B0E28BA4FC08F12F33F339D27FA25": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Early Childhood Special Education and Bilingual Education",
      "description": "Assistant Professor in Early Childhood Special Education and Bilingual Education. Assistant Professor in Early Childhood Special Education and Bilingual Education **FACULTY VACANCY ANNOUNCEMENT** **Position Details** The Department of Interdisciplinary Arts & Sciences at the City College Center for Worker Education (CUNY) invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position in Early Childhood Special Education/Bilingual Education, beginning in Fall 2027. Candidates should demonstrate active engagement in research, teaching, and community service, and be able to prepare early childhood educators and teach methods courses in early childhood special education and early childhood bilingual education. The successful candidate will also demonstrate enthusiasm and ability to collaborate across divisions and programs within City College. The department offers an interdisciplinary BA in Liberal Arts, a BA in Social Justice and Urban Life, a BS in Early Childhood Education for working adults, an MA in the Study of the Americas, and two new MA programs in Instructional Design and International and Global Education. The BS degree is offered jointly with the City College School of Education. The position will involve participation in some School of Education meetings, as well as data collection and analysis for accreditation bodies. **Responsibilities** The successful candidate will: + Teach 45 courses (18 credit hours) per academic year. + Mentor and advise undergraduate and graduate students. + Work with MA students and contribute to the development of a growing curriculum in Early Childhood Education, International and Global Education, and Instructional Design. + Build and sustain community partnerships. + Engage in research and scholarly activities in areas of expertise. + Share responsibility for departmental and college committee service. + Perform administrative and supervisory duties as assigned. **QUALIFICATIONS** **QUALIFICATIONS** Ph.D. or Ed.D in a relevant field or equivalent. Also required are the ability to teach successfully, demonstrated scholarship or achievement, and ability to cooperate with others for the good of the institution. **Preferred Qualifications** + Ph.D. or Ed.D. with specialization in Early Childhood Education and Early Childhood Bilingual Special Education + Availability to work evening and weekend hours. + Experience working full-time with children from birth through 2nd grade. + Experience teaching in special education and bilingual education programs. + Ability to teach successfully in an urban setting. + Commitment to teacher education, with a demonstrated record of scholarship and an active research agenda. + Experience in applying contemporary adult education practices, innovative teaching modalities, and instructional technologies is especially encouraged. **COMPENSATION** **Salary Range:** $79,630 - $94,017 CUNY offers faculty a competitive compensation and benefits package covering health insurance, pension and retirement benefits, paid parental leave, and savings programs. We also provide mentoring and support for research, scholarship, and publication as part of our commitment to ongoing faculty professional development. **HOW TO APPLY** **Only applications submitted through CUNYfirst will be considered for this position.** If you are viewing this job posting in CUNYfirst, please click on \"Apply Now\" on the bottom of this page and follow the instructions. If you are viewing this job posting externally, please apply as follows: + Go to https://cuny.jobs/ + Search for Job Opening ID number **32776** + Click on the \"Apply Now\" button and follow the instructions. Applications, including the following must be uploaded to the CUNYfirst job application website as a single PDF document: 1. Statement of scholarly interests; 2. Curriculum Vitae / Resume; 3. Names and contact information for three professional references **CLOSING DATE** November 15, 2026 **JOB SEARCH CATEGORY** CUNY Job Posting: Faculty **EQUAL EMPLOYMENT OPPORTUNITY** CUNY encourages people with disabilities, minorities, veterans and women to apply. At CUNY, Italian Americans are also included among our protected groups. Applicants and employees will not be discriminated against on the basis of any legally protected category, including sexual orientation or gender identity. EEO/AA/Vet/Disability Employer. Job ID: 32776 Location: City College of New York Job Type: Full-Time"
    },
    "https://cuny.jobs/1FE0AF63289849998A49A1438CF83EC625": {
      "title": "Associate Professor of Philosophy - Arthur Zitrin Professorship in Bioethics",
      "description": "Associate Professor of Philosophy - Arthur Zitrin Professorship in Bioethics. Associate Professor of Philosophy - Arthur Zitrin Professorship in Bioethics **FACULTY VACANCY ANNOUNCEMENT** **About the City College of New York** For over 175 years, The City College of New York has provided access to high-quality education for New Yorkers in a wide variety of disciplines. Over 13,000 students pursue undergraduate and graduate degrees in the College of Liberal Arts and Sciences, the School of Architecture, the School of Education, the Grove School of Engineering, the Center for Worker Education, the Colin Powell School for Civic and Global Leadership, and the Sophie Davis School of Biomedical Education. The City College of New York has produced ten Nobel laureates. **About the City College of New York Philosophy Department** The Philosophy Department has nine full-time faculty members, 100 majors and 60 minors, an active Philosophy Club, and frequent departmental events, including the Philosophy Pizza Forum. **Position Details** The Department of Philosophy at The City College of New York (CCNY) of the City University of New York (CUNY) invites applications for the Arthur Zitrin Professorship in Bioethics, an endowed position to be filled at the rank of Associate Professor, anticipated to begin in Fall 2027. The position will involve research, undergraduate teaching at the introductory level and in the applicants areas of expertise, and departmental service. The Zitrin Professorship will provide support of $10,000-$40,000 in addition to the salary range specified below, depending on experience and qualifications. Area of Specialization: Bioethics Area of Competence: Open **QUALIFICATIONS** Ph.D. degree in Philosophy. Also required are the ability to teach successfully, demonstrated scholarship or achievement, and ability to cooperate with others for the good of the institution. **Preferred Qualifications** + Evidence of excellence in research, teaching, and departmental service. + Preferred but not required: experience in administering programs for students, such as internships, and/or experience with grants that could support such programs. **COMPENSATION** Salary Range: $94,017 - $113,745 The Zitrin Professorship will provide support of $10,000-$40,000 in addition to the salary range, depending on experience and qualifications. CUNY offers faculty a competitive compensation and benefits package covering health insurance, pension and retirement benefits, paid parental leave, and savings programs. We also provide mentoring and support for research, scholarship, and publication as part of our commitment to ongoing faculty professional development. **HOW TO APPLY** **Only applications submitted through CUNYfirst will be considered for this position.** If you are viewing this job posting in CUNYfirst, please click on \"Apply Now\" on the bottom of this page and follow the instructions. If you are viewing this job posting externally, please apply as follows: + Go to https://cuny.jobs/ + Search for Job Opening ID number **32774** + Click on the \"Apply Now\" button and follow the instructions. Applications, including the following, must be uploaded to the CUNYfirst job application website as a single PDF document: 1. Cover Letter; 2. Curriculum Vitae 3. Summaries of recent student teaching evaluations; 4. At least one article-length writing sample, and no more than three; 5. Contact information (including email address) for at least three people willing to provide letters of recommendation. **CLOSING DATE** November 15, 2026 **JOB SEARCH CATEGORY** CUNY Job Posting: Faculty **EQUAL EMPLOYMENT OPPORTUNITY** CUNY encourages people with disabilities, minorities, veterans and women to apply. At CUNY, Italian Americans are also included among our protected groups. Applicants and employees will not be discriminated against on the basis of any legally protected category, including sexual orientation or gender identity. EEO/AA/Vet/Disability Employer. Job ID: 32774 Location: City College of New York Job Type: Full-Time"
    },
    "https://cuny.jobs/C09D3C2DFF604DD0BA1D17AF434888B225": {
      "title": "Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor (Tenure-track or Tenure) - Architecture and Urban AI",
      "description": "Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor (Tenure-track or Tenure) - Architecture and Urban AI. Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor (Tenure-track or Tenure) - Architecture and Urban AI **FACULTY VACANCY ANNOUNCEMENT** The Bernard and Anne Spitzer School of Architecture at the City College of New York is pleased to announce a faculty search as part of the CUNY Martin S. Spergel Initiative in Computational Science supported by the Simons Foundation. The Spitzer School seeks colleague, preferably mid to senior career who will take a leading role in defining the interdisciplinary Spergel cluster, Urban AI: Intelligent Systems for Sustainability and Planning in Urban Settings. The field of specialty for this full-time tenured or tenure-track position is artificial intelligence and physical design in the urban built environment, engaging emerging fields such as geospatial AI, cyber-physical systems, robotics, or physical AI. This faculty appointment will commence in Fall 2027 and includes teaching, research, and service responsibilities allied with Spitzers undergraduate and graduate programs in architecture, landscape architecture, urban design, and urban studies along with teaching responsibility in the CUNY Graduate Center. Through the Martin S. Spergel Initiative in Computational Sciences, the position carries a substantial startup package. We seek a cutting-edge, imaginative scholar whose research builds new computational methods, tools, and theories for design, who understands AI as a creative force in shaping the built environment, and who grapples with the ethical questions that AI raises. The successful candidate will direct this work toward urgent urban challenges, including sustainability, climate resilience, and social equity. As an intellectual leader of the Urban AI cluster, this person will develop, define, and shape its research and pedagogical agendas, and shape the long-term vision of the initiative in partnership with colleagues at the Spitzer School, the City College of New York, Queens College, and the CUNY Graduate Center. Although interdisciplinary collaboration is expected, this position is seated in the architecture department at the Spitzer School. A dedicated teacher is needed who is committed to teaching artificial intelligence and computational methods to design students at the undergraduate and graduate levels. The appointee will also contribute to the MS in Computational Sciences at the CUNY Graduate Center. The successful candidate will join a vibrant research community at the Spitzer School, one that is supported by City Labs, a consortium made up of the Robotics Laboratory with industrial robots, the Advanced Building Construction Laboratory, focused on decarbonization, and the Living Systems Laboratory. The J. Max Bond Center for Urban Futures offers further expertise in urban data analytics with a focus on community engagement. The Spitzer School is committed to social and sustainable concerns in design and practice, as well as to providing access to the finest education in the art, history, and technology of architecture, landscape architecture, urban design, and urban studies to the broadest and most diverse student population. A prospective member of the faculty should be prepared to participate in and contribute to this lively intellectual atmosphere and join the SSA in its commitment in furthering justice, equity, diversity, and inclusion within the academy and the design professions. Expect to share responsibility for committee and department assignments including administrative, supervisory, and other functions. **QUALIFICATIONS** **For Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor:** Ph.D. degree in area(s) of experience or equivalent. Also required are the ability to teach successfully, demonstrated scholarship or achievement, and ability to cooperate with others for the good of the institution. **Preferred Qualifications** + A PhD in architecture, design computation, planning, or a closely related field and a terminal degree in design (architecture or landscape architecture). Completion of the PhD is required before the start date in late August 2027. + A demonstrated research agenda applying artificial intelligence or computational methods to design and the urban built environment. + A record of teaching at the undergraduate and/or graduate level in design studios, seminars, and/or lecture courses, including the ability to teach AI and computational methods to design students. + A sustained record of research, publication, and external funding sufficient for appointment at the rank of assistant, associate, or full professor. + Expertise in one or more emerging areas such as geospatial AI, cyber-physical systems, robotics, or physical AI + Evidence of leading funded projects and multidisciplinary research teams + Experience mentoring junior faculty and student researchers + Commitment to equity, sustainability, and community-engaged scholarship, and to teaching a diverse student body + A collaborative, team-oriented approach suited to building and leading an interdisciplinary research cluster **COMPENSATION** Assistant Professor: $89,777 - $112,841 Associate Professor: $109,519 $133,558 Full Professor: $130,755 $160,827 CUNY offers faculty a competitive compensation and benefits package covering health insurance, pension and retirement benefits, paid parental leave, and savings programs. We also provide mentoring and support for research, scholarship, and publication as part of our commitment to ongoing faculty professional development. **HOW TO APPLY** **Only applications submitted through CUNYfirst will be considered for this position.** If you are viewing this job posting in CUNYfirst, please click on \"Apply Now\" on the bottom of this page and follow the instructions. If you are viewing this job posting externally, please apply as follows: + Go to https://cuny.jobs/ + Search for Job Opening ID number **32771** + Click on the \"Apply Now\" button and follow the instructions. Applications, including the following must be uploaded to the CUNYfirst job application website as a single PDF document: 1. Cover letter explaining interest in the position and qualifications (including notable accomplishments in design and research), research agenda, and teaching philosophy (maximum 3 pages); 2. Curriculum Vitae, including a publication list; 3. A sample course syllabus; 4. Two sample publications (no more than 50 pages); 5. Digital portfolio of representative research, design work, and teaching (maximum 10 MB); 6. Names and contact information of three (3) professional academic references, noting your relationship to each one. **CLOSING DATE** November 15, 2026 **JOB SEARCH CATEGORY** CUNY Job Posting: Faculty **EQUAL EMPLOYMENT OPPORTUNITY** CUNY encourages people with disabilities, minorities, veterans and women to apply. At CUNY, Italian Americans are also included among our protected groups. Applicants and employees will not be discriminated against on the basis of any legally protected category, including sexual orientation or gender identity. EEO/AA/Vet/Disability Employer. Job ID: 32771 Location: City College of New York Job Type: Full-Time"
    },
    "https://cuny.jobs/5E08ECE6D3AE4AABA4ABF4C517ABD94B25": {
      "title": "Associate Professor - Urban Studies (Tenure - Track)",
      "description": "Associate Professor - Urban Studies (Tenure - Track). Associate Professor - Urban Studies (Tenure - Track) **FACULTY VACANCY ANNOUNCEMENT** **Position Details** The Bernard and Anne Spitzer School of Architecture (SSA) seeks applications for a full-time, tenure-track position in urban studies at the entering rank of associate professor, starting Fall 2027. We seek an expert on public housing in urban environments whose research and teaching situates urban studies in global, transregional and/or cross-cultural contexts. The successful candidate will teach in our B.A. in Urban Studies and the Built Environment program, offering the required courses Introduction to Urban Studies, Techniques of Urban Communication, and Social Justice and the Built Environment. They will also advise majors in a required two semester capstone project. The ideal candidate will also be willing and able to teach a required course on the built environment of New York City for first-year students in SSA's professional B.Arch. program. The faculty member will have the opportunity to teach advanced seminars on public housing and other topics related to area of specialization. The SSA is committed to social and sustainable concerns in design and practice, as well as to providing access to the finest education in the art, history, technology of architecture, landscape architecture, urban design, and urban studies to the broadest and most diverse student population. A prospective member of the faculty should be prepared to participate in and contribute to this lively intellectual atmosphere, and join the SSA in its commitment in furthering justice, equity, diversity, inclusion, and anti-racism within the academy and the design professions. The Associate Professor will share responsibility for committee and department assignments including administrative, supervisory, and other functions. **QUALIFICATIONS** Ph.D. urban studies, urban geography, architectural history or a related field. Also required are the ability to teach successfully, demonstrated scholarship or achievement, and ability to cooperate with others for the good of the institution. **Preferred Qualifications** + The candidate must have experience in teaching undergraduate lecture courses and/or seminars in urban studies. + Scholarship appropriate for an appointment at the rank of associate professor. + A deep commitment to city life and the fundamental principles of a public institution of higher education with a highly diverse student body is expected. **COMPENSATION** **Salary Range:** $109,519 $133,558 CUNY offers faculty a competitive compensation and benefits package covering health insurance, pension and retirement benefits, paid parental leave, and savings programs. We also provide mentoring and support for research, scholarship, and publication as part of our commitment to ongoing faculty professional development. **HOW TO APPLY** **Only applications submitted through CUNYfirst will be considered for this position.** If you are viewing this job posting in CUNYfirst, please click on \"Apply Now\" on the bottom of this page and follow the instructions. If you are viewing this job posting externally, please apply as follows: + Go to https://cuny.jobs/ + Search for Job Opening ID number **32798** + Click on the \"Apply Now\" button and follow the instructions. Applications, including the following must be uploaded to the CUNYfirst job application website as a single PDF document: + Curriculum Vitae; + Cover Letter explaining interest in position, teaching philosophy, and scholarly objectives; Candidates completing the Ph.D. at the time of application should specify the dates of their doctoral dissertation defense and degree conferral; + One sample course syllabus; + Two sample publications (50 pp. maximum in total); + The names and contact information for three (3) professional academic references **CLOSING DATE** Open until filled with review to begin November 21, 2026. **JOB SEARCH CATEGORY** CUNY Job Posting: Faculty **EQUAL EMPLOYMENT OPPORTUNITY** CUNY encourages people with disabilities, minorities, veterans and women to apply. At CUNY, Italian Americans are also included among our protected groups. Applicants and employees will not be discriminated against on the basis of any legally protected category, including sexual orientation or gender identity. EEO/AA/Vet/Disability Employer. Job ID: 32798 Location: City College of New York Job Type: Full-Time"
    },
    "https://cuny.jobs/9A030FF2474C49068AF5EE4CFF753DF625": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor (Tenure-track) â€“ Architectural History and Urban Studies",
      "description": "Assistant Professor (Tenure-track) â€“ Architectural History and Urban Studies. Assistant Professor (Tenure-track) Architectural History and Urban Studies **FACULTY VACANCY ANNOUNCEMENT** The Bernard and Anne Spitzer School of Architecture (SSA) seeks applications for a full-time, tenure-track position in architectural history and urban studies at the entering rank of assistant professor, starting Fall 2027. The candidates research and teaching will focus on the 20th and 21st centuries, and situate architectural history, urban history, and urban studies in global, transregional, and/or cross-cultural contexts. The successful candidate will teach in our B.A. in Urban Studies and the Built Environment program, offering the required lecture courses Introduction to Urban Studies and the City in History, and advise majors in a required two-semester capstone project. The successful candidate will also teach a global history of 20th- and 21st-century architecture in the SSAs professional Bachelor and Master of Architecture programs. The faculty member will have the opportunity to teach advanced seminars in the area of specialization. The SSA is committed to social and sustainable concerns in design and practice, as well as to providing access to the finest education in the art, history, and technology of architecture, landscape architecture, urban design, and urban studies to the broadest and most diverse student population. A prospective member of the faculty should be prepared to participate in and contribute to this lively intellectual atmosphere, and join the SSA in its commitment in furthering justice, equity, diversity, inclusion, and anti-racism within the academy and the design professions. The Assistant Professor will share responsibility for committee and department assignments including administrative, supervisory, and other functions. **QUALIFICATIONS** Ph.D. degree in architectural history, urban history or equivalent. Also required are the ability to teach successfully, demonstrated scholarship or achievement, and ability to cooperate with others for the good of the institution. **Preferred Qualifications** + Conferral of the Ph.D. is required before the start date in late August 2027. + The candidate must have experience in teaching undergraduate and/or graduate lecture courses and seminars in architectural history and/or urban studies. + Scholarship appropriate for an appointment at the rank of assistant professor. + A deep commitment to city life and the fundamental principles of a public institution of higher education with a highly diverse student body is expected. **COMPENSATION** **Salary Range:** $89,777 - $112,841. Salary commensurate with education and experience. CUNY offers faculty a competitive compensation and benefits package covering health insurance, pension and retirement benefits, paid parental leave, and savings programs. We also provide mentoring and support for research, scholarship, and publication as part of our commitment to ongoing faculty professional development. **HOW TO APPLY** **Only applications submitted through CUNYfirst will be considered for this position.** If you are viewing this job posting in CUNYfirst, please click on \"Apply Now\" on the bottom of this page and follow the instructions. If you are viewing this job posting externally, please apply as follows: + Go to https://cuny.jobs/ + Search for Job Opening ID number **32784** + Click on the \"Apply Now\" button and follow the instructions. Applications, including the following must be uploaded to the CUNYfirst job application website as a single PDF document: 1. Curriculum Vitae; 2. Cover Letter explaining interest in position, teaching philosophy, and scholarly objectives. Candidates completing the Ph.D. at the time of application should specify the dates of their doctoral dissertation defense and degree conferral; 3. One sample course syllabus; 4. Two sample publications (50 pp. maximum in total); 5. The names and contact information for three (3) professional academic references. **CLOSING DATE** November 24, 2026 **JOB SEARCH CATEGORY** CUNY Job Posting: Faculty **EQUAL EMPLOYMENT OPPORTUNITY** CUNY encourages people with disabilities, minorities, veterans and women to apply. At CUNY, Italian Americans are also included among our protected groups. Applicants and employees will not be discriminated against on the basis of any legally protected category, including sexual orientation or gender identity. EEO/AA/Vet/Disability Employer. Job ID: 32784 Location: City College of New York Job Type: Full-Time"
    },
    "https://cuny.jobs/42EB8434962F4FB6BB6047378D3C740325": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Literacy and Early Childhood Education",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Literacy and Early Childhood Education. Assistant Professor of Literacy and Early Childhood Education **FACULTY VACANCY ANNOUNCEMENT** **Position Details** The School of Education at The City College of New York invites applications for a full-time, Assistant Professor position in Literacy and Early Childhood Education to begin in the Fall of 2027. The position requires service to the department and college and academic advisement. The successful candidate is expected to collaborate with colleagues to seek external funding, to collaborate with local school districts, to teach literacy courses and courses in the Early Childhood Education program, and to participate in committee work. In addition, the successful candidate is expected to design and eventually direct or co-direct a graduate P-12 literacy program. **QUALIFICATIONS** Ph.D. or Ed.D. in Literacy, Reading, Language Education, or a related field. Also required are the ability to teach successfully, demonstrated scholarship or achievement, and ability to cooperate with others for the good of the institution. **Preferred Qualifications** + Experience teaching or working in an official capacity in public schools, daycare centers, or community-based early childhood education settings. + Experience teaching face-to-face, online, and hybrid courses. + Experience teaching undergraduate and graduate courses. + Commitment to urban education + Ability to teach courses in other areas, preferably childhood education or special education. **COMPENSATION** **Salary Range:** $79,630 - $89,777 CUNY offers faculty a competitive compensation and benefits package covering health insurance, pension and retirement benefits, paid parental leave, and savings programs. We also provide mentoring and support for research, scholarship, and publication as part of our commitment to ongoing faculty professional development. **HOW TO APPLY** **Only applications submitted through CUNYfirst will be considered for this position.** If you are viewing this job posting in CUNYfirst, please click on \"Apply Now\" on the bottom of this page and follow the instructions. If you are viewing this job posting externally, please apply as follows: + Go to https://cuny.jobs/ + Search for Job Opening ID number **32796** + Click on the \"Apply Now\" button and follow the instructions. Applications, including the following must be uploaded to the CUNYfirst job application website as a single PDF document: + Letter of Interest: This letter should include career highlights, teaching experience, and alignment with CCNYs mission. We encourage applicants to discuss how their scholarship and/or teaching philosophies demonstrate a commitment to fostering inclusive approaches to teaching and mentoring pre-service and in-service teachers. + CV; + List of three references with contact information; + Samples of other expository writing are welcome but not required. **CLOSING DATE** November 22, 2026 **JOB SEARCH CATEGORY** CUNY Job Posting: Faculty **EQUAL EMPLOYMENT OPPORTUNITY** CUNY encourages people with disabilities, minorities, veterans and women to apply. At CUNY, Italian Americans are also included among our protected groups. Applicants and employees will not be discriminated against on the basis of any legally protected category, including sexual orientation or gender identity. EEO/AA/Vet/Disability Employer. Job ID: 32796 Location: City College of New York Job Type: Full-Time"
    }
